// `pilot debug --dap` (PROP-040 §3): a whole session over the program
// tests/tool_fixtures/dap/sample.pilot, with the requests on standard input
// (dap_session.stdin, one JSON body per line; lib/dap_client.pilot frames
// them and prints a digest of each message the debugger sends).
//
// Expected, worked by hand from the fixture and the DAP specification:
//   initialize is answered, then the `initialized` event. launch and a
//   breakpoint on line 9 (`let y = add(x, 4);`, which has code) are
//   accepted; configurationDone starts the run, which stops at line 9
//   (reason breakpoint) before that line runs.
//   Frames are numbered by depth from 1: the start function the lowering
//   adds is frame 1 (hidden: it has no name), main frame 2, a call from
//   main frame 3; each frame's locals are scope reference = its frame id.
//   At line 9 only x (line 8, done) is in scope: x = 3. y's statement has
//   not finished.
//   stepIn stops at the first line of add (line 3) with a = 3, b = 4 (sum
//   not yet); the stack is add line 3 over main line 9.
//   stepOut finishes add and stops at the next new line of main, line 10
//   (`print(y);`): x = 3, y = 3 + 4 = 7. evaluate `y` gives 7.
//   next runs line 10 (output 7) and stops at line 11.
//   continue runs to the end: z = 7 * 2 = 14 is printed, main returns
//   14 - 11 = 3, the exit code. disconnect is answered; the debugger itself
//   exits 0. Messages are numbered 1, 2, 3, .. in the order they are sent.
use lib.dap_client;

fn main() {
    run_and_digest("tests/tool_fixtures/dap/sample.pilot");
}
